course: biology
lesson: 235
title: Animal minds: dolphins' names, orca grandmothers and whale songs
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Dolphins and whales are mammals that went back to the sea about 50 million years ago, and they took big, social brains with them.
explain: A bottlenose dolphin has its own whistle that works like a name, and its friends call it by copying that whistle.
explain: Orca families are led by old grandmothers who remember where to find salmon in hard years, and humpback whales share songs that change like pop hits.
explain: Watch a family at the beach: the kids learn tricks from older kids and grandma knows where the best rock pools are; dolphin and orca groups work in much the same way.
fact: Each bottlenose dolphin has its own signature whistle, invented as a calf, which others copy to call it, and dolphins remember old friends' whistles for over 20 years.
fact: Dolphins hunt as a team, herding fish into a tight bait ball and taking turns to dive through it; some in Florida slap mud rings so fish jump into their mouths.
fact: In Shark Bay, Australia, some dolphins wear sea sponges on their snouts to protect them while they poke in the sand, and mothers pass the trick to their young; a habit learned from others is called culture.
fact: Orcas in Antarctica swim side by side to make a wave that washes a seal off an ice floe, and older orcas teach younger ones.
fact: Different orca groups eat different food and use different call «dialects», and they do not mix.
fact: Orca groups are led by old grandmothers who can live 80 or even 90 years and remember where salmon are in bad years.
fact: Male humpbacks sing long songs that can last 20 minutes and be repeated for hours, and the song slowly changes over the years and spreads across oceans.
fact: There are real reports of dolphins staying near swimmers in danger, but dolphins are wild animals with big teeth and can be rough.
fact: Dolphins rest one half of their brain at a time, keeping the other half awake to breathe and watch out, because a dolphin must choose to come up and breathe or it would drown, with the eye on the awake side open.
fact: Dolphins blow rings of air underwater and play with them, pushing and splitting them, and humpbacks blow spiral bubble nets to trap fish.
fact: In tests in Hawaii, dolphins understood that word order in hand-signal sentences changes the meaning.
fact: Many people think tanks are too small for such far-swimming animals, and some countries have banned dolphin shows.
fact: The closest living land relatives of whales are hippos, and a whale's flipper has the same bones as your arm and hand.
exercise: What is a dolphin's signature whistle? | Its own whistle that works like a name | A song that all the dolphins in the sea share | A warning of sharks only | A noise made by its tail
exercise: How long can dolphins remember an old friend's whistle? | Over 20 years | About one day only | About a week or so | They forget it at once
exercise: How do dolphins catch fish in a bait ball? | They herd the fish together and take turns diving through | They wait for the fish to fall asleep and then gulp them all at once | Each dolphin hunts alone far away | They spit water at the fish
exercise: Why do some Shark Bay dolphins wear sponges on their snouts? | To protect their snouts while poking in the sand | To look pretty for the other dolphins in the bay | To keep their noses warm | To soak up fresh water so they can drink it
exercise: Who teaches the sponge trick? | Mothers, to their young | Aquarium trainers who visit the bay | Nobody, every dolphin is born knowing it | The oldest males in the group
exercise: What is culture in animals? | A habit learned from others, not from instinct | Any habit that an animal is born already knowing | A kind of food | The place where an animal lives
exercise: How do Antarctic orcas get a seal off an ice floe? | They swim side by side to make a wave that washes it off | They break the ice with their teeth | They wait until the seal falls asleep and slips into the water | They call the seal down
exercise: Who leads an orca group? | An old grandmother | The biggest young male | The newest calf in the group | A different orca every day
exercise: Why does an orca grandmother matter in a bad year? | She remembers where salmon can be found | She catches all of the fish for the group herself | She keeps the group warm | She scares away the big whales
exercise: How long can a humpback song last? | About 20 minutes, repeated for hours | About two seconds | A whole year without a single break for breath | Humpbacks do not sing
exercise: What happens to humpback songs over the years? | They slowly change and spread, like a pop hit | They stay exactly the same for ever and ever | Each whale sings a different song | They get shorter until they stop
exercise: How does a dolphin sleep? | It rests one half of its brain at a time | It sleeps on the sea floor for hours | It never rests at all | It floats with both eyes shut all night
exercise: Why can a dolphin not fall fully asleep? | It must choose to breathe or it would drown | It is afraid of the dark water at night | Its eyes cannot close | It has no brain halves to rest in turn
exercise: What do dolphins do with bubble rings? | Push and split them in play | Breathe the air from inside them | Trap little fish inside them to eat | Nothing, they just swim past them
exercise: What did dolphins understand in tests in Hawaii? | That word order in hand-signal sentences changes the meaning | How to write words | Spoken English, the way the trainers talked to each other every day | How to count to a thousand
exercise: Why do some people object to dolphin shows? | Tanks are tiny for clever animals that swim far | Dolphins hate fish | Shows are too quiet | Dolphins cannot learn any tricks at all, so they are made to suffer
exercise: What are whales' closest living land relatives? | Hippos | Big sharks | Fur seals | Elephants
test: In a cartoon a dolphin rescues a drowning boy and gives him a ride home. What is the real picture? | Dolphins have stayed near swimmers in danger, but they are wild and can be rough | Dolphins always save every swimmer | Dolphins never come near people | Dolphins carry people about as pets and take them home whenever they get tired of swimming
test: «dolphin's sleeping with one eye open, is it spying» What is the best answer? | One half of its brain rests while the other half stays awake | It is pretending to sleep to trick the sharks into coming close | Its eyelid is broken | Dolphins never sleep
test: «Jake reckons humpbacks just moo randomly» What is closer to the truth? | Males sing long songs that a whole area shares and changes | Humpbacks only make one sound | Humpbacks are silent | Only the baby humpbacks sing, to call their mothers when they get lost
test: What would happen to an orca family if its old grandmother died before a bad salmon year? | They might struggle to find food | Nothing, every orca knows everything | They would all become vegetarian | They would swim to a lake
test: «a whale is just a massive fish innit» What is wrong with that? | Whales are mammals that came from land animals | Nothing, whales are fish | Whales are giant sharks | Whales are close relatives of octopuses and squid
test: Your arm and a whale's flipper. What do they share? | The same set of bones, shaped differently | Nothing at all | Feathers | Gills
test: Count them: a pod of 7 dolphins swims by, and each one has one blowhole and two flippers. How many flippers? | 14 flippers | 7 flippers | 21 flippers | 28 flippers
test: A trainer shows «ball take to hoop», then «hoop take to ball». What does a trained dolphin do? | Two different things, because word order changes the meaning | The same thing both times | Nothing, the signs are too hard | Swims away from both, because the two signs mean the opposite
test: Two orca groups meet in the same sea. One eats fish, the other eats seals. What usually happens? | They keep apart and use different call dialects | They join up at once | They swap food every day | They become one family
test: «mum, why are those dolphins slapping the mud in a circle» What is going on? | They trap fish, which jump out of the ring into their mouths | They are angry at the mud | They are building a nest | They are washing themselves clean in the mud, like pigs
test: A humpback blows bubbles in a spiral under a shoal of fish. What is it doing? | Making a bubble net to trap the fish | Playing with bubble rings, just for fun | Breathing out slowly after a long sleep | Cleaning out its blowhole with bubbles
exam: Identify the function of the signature whistle of the bottlenose dolphin. | It serves as an individual identifier comparable to a name | It serves exclusively as an alarm in respect of approaching sharks | It is identical in every member of the species | It is produced solely during sleep
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the leadership of an orca group? | The group is led by an aged female whose memory of food sources aids its survival | The group is led by the youngest calf | The group possesses no leader whatsoever | The group is led by a different whale each day, chosen by the strength of its calls
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the use of sponges by dolphins in Shark Bay. | The practice is transmitted from mother to offspring and constitutes culture | The practice is innate in every dolphin | The practice was introduced by human trainers at a nearby marine park | The practice serves to warm the snout
exam: Ascertain the manner in which a dolphin obtains rest whilst continuing to breathe. | By resting one hemisphere of the brain at a time | By descending to the sea floor for several hours | By ceasing to breathe throughout the night | By attaching itself to floating ice
